  "note": "RFC 4271 Section 3.2 requires every Loc-RIB route's next hop to be resolvable through the local Routing Table. Independently read the six tagged CI covers, both real TCP ingress scenarios, their selection/Loc-RIB/kernel observers and the actual admission-to-installation producers. The ordinary internal and forked carriers prove that an unresolved higher-LOCAL_PREF input cannot displace a resolved zero-cost alternate, that loss of both covers removes the retained subject from BGP best, Loc-RIB and every Linux routing table, and that a direct BGP control survives. Restoration uses retained input without another UPDATE. The prospective carriers first install three genuine BGP seeds, establish ordinary resolution of each replacement's next hop, then require withdrawal and absence of the direct and indirectly self-capturing subjects while preserving exact received attributes. The longer interior host route independently resolves the protected replacement, which must remain selected and installed alongside the direct control. Both prospective carriers completed 80 distinct passing runs with all 1120 verification steps each. Read all six accepted 3.1-2 clean/fault receipts and the current native freshness report: all six covers are fresh, with no stale or unproven cover. The ordinary admission-bypass faults fail the initial subject election at 150/24 via unresolved 10.250.1.1; the current-only prospective faults fail the first direct 152/24 forbidden best publication; the over-rejecting containment faults lose protected 154/24 while 151/24 remains visible. The early best-publication failures also violate this Loc-RIB requirement by source coupling: checkRouteBestChange mirrors the selected path before its caller publishes the best change; internal insertion is synchronous, and forked insertion flushes RouteInstall through applyRouteInstall into engine Loc-RIB before returning. With the actual default import configuration, Path.Valid and admit do not add a reachability gate, and upsert retains the selected BGP contribution. FIB rejection cannot repair that Loc-RIB admission. The forked diagnostics show downstream unreachable-route installation before the intended observer failure; route-remove/connection warnings follow failure during shutdown and are not substituted for the semantic failure. This inference is bounded to successful ordinary installation transport and the tested default configuration, not arbitrary RPC failure or rejecting import policy. No direct later Loc-RIB/kernel assertion failure, separately broken indirect case, or separately broken loss/restoration outcome is claimed: fatal primary assertions stop those broken runs. The clean lifecycle assertions and exact protected/direct controls remain mandatory. The bound helper and coupling inventory prevents a later observer, ordering or admission change from retaining this assurance unnoticed.",
